#!/usr/sbin/nft -f
#
# Firewall for LXC `wiki-mirror` (the Wikipedia mirror). THIS FILE IS A TEMPLATE.
#
# The three values that differ between sites - the LAN, the embedding server and its port - are
# not written here. install.sh reads them from /etc/wiki-mirror/site.env (deploy/site.env.example),
# validates each against a strict IPv4/CIDR/port pattern, and substitutes them into the three
# `define` lines at the top of the table, which are the only lines with a placeholder. It
# installs the result as /etc/nftables.d/wiki-mirror.nft and refuses to apply anything that still
# holds a placeholder. The rest of the file is the same everywhere. Do not load this file with
# `nft -f` as it stands.
#
# This is the only box that downloads from Wikimedia, so unlike a client box's locked-down
# baseline it must reach the internet - but only as the
# two identities that need to, and never as the API. Default-deny both directions:
#
#   INPUT   SSH and the API port (8300) from the LAN, loopback, replies, ICMP. Everything on
#           8300 needs a bearer key (the main client's, and one per MCP agent), so the LAN is
#           the boundary, not the authorisation.
#   OUTPUT  Per-user (`meta skuid`), because the destinations cannot be listed:
#             wikimirror-sync  tcp 443 anywhere (Enterprise API + auth, and the rotating S3
#                              addresses the chunk downloads redirect to), the embedding
#                              server (W2 embeddings; its address and port are site.env's).
#             root             tcp 80/443 anywhere (PyPI, python.org, GitHub releases),
#                              git-over-SSH to GitHub's published ranges (set github_git),
#                              and the DHCP lease renewal to the LAN (this box is
#                              `eth0 inet dhcp`, and ifupdown's dhclient runs as root).
#             _apt             tcp 80/443 anywhere. apt does not fetch as root: it drops to
#                              its sandbox user `_apt` (uid 42) for every download, so
#                              `apt-get update` as root was refused until this rule existed.
#                              Debian's stock sources use plain-http mirrors
#                              (signature-verified; the transport is not what is trusted),
#                              hence 80 as well as 443.
#             wikimirror       nothing. The API serves reads from local disk and Qdrant on
#                              loopback; if it ever tries to open a connection that is a
#                              compromise, so it is counted and refused.
#             qdrant           nothing, for the same reason.
#             everyone         DNS/NTP to the LAN resolver, and nothing else.
#
#         **Refused, not dropped.** A connection nothing admits gets a TCP reset (or an ICMP
#         admin-prohibited for anything else) instead of silence, so a client fails at once.
#         This matters on this box: it has only a ULA IPv6 address (no routed v6), and a
#         resolver that answers an AAAA record makes a client try v6 first; a silent drop made
#         every such attempt wait out a 30 s connect timeout before falling back to IPv4.
#
# A compromised parse job (wikimirror-sync reads attacker-editable HTML) can still send data to
# any host on 443. That is the accepted cost of S3's rotating addresses; what it cannot do is
# reach the LAN (only the embedding server) or run as a user with the API key. Wikipedia text holds
# nothing secret, and the Enterprise sign-in file is readable by wikimirror-sync alone.
#
# **Lockout safety.** Established flows are accepted first, so the SSH session that applies
# this file survives; new SSH is accepted from the LAN (LAN_CIDR in site.env) by a rule above
# the final drop. A LAN_CIDR that does not contain your workstation therefore refuses every NEW
# connection from it once applied; the installer checks only that the resolver is inside it.
# If a mistake ever does lock the LAN out: from the Proxmox host, `pct enter <ctid>`, then
# `nft flush ruleset`. The container console never depends on this ruleset.
#
# **Apply** with deploy/install.sh (renders this template with site.env, installs the result
# into /etc/nftables.d, ensures the include in /etc/nftables.conf, runs `nft -c` on the whole
# ruleset, then applies it; if the check fails it puts the previous file back, so the next boot
# never loads a ruleset that failed. `install.sh --show-firewall` prints the rendered ruleset and
# changes nothing). Never `cp` + `nft -f` by hand: this file is a template, and it names system users,
# and nft refuses the whole file over one unknown name (install.sh creates them first).
# **Verify** with `nft list counters` (what was refused) and `wiki-mirror status`.
#
# The file declares then deletes its own table before redefining it, so loading it twice -
# by hand, by the installer, or at boot after /etc/nftables.conf's `flush ruleset` - never
# duplicates a rule.
#
# IPv6: nothing but link-local neighbour discovery is allowed either way; no rule here
# accepts a routed IPv6 address.
#
# See docs/DESIGN.md section 6 and docs/OPERATIONS.md.

table inet wiki_mirror
delete table inet wiki_mirror

table inet wiki_mirror {

    # The three site values (see the header): the only lines install.sh edits, each replaced by
    # a literal it has validated. Everything below refers to them by name.
    #
    # LAN prefix the embedding server, the clients, the NAS and the Proxmox host live on.
    define LAN_V4 = @LAN_CIDR@
    # The Ollama-compatible embedding server, for the W2 embedding job (docs/DESIGN.md section 5).
    define EMBEDDING_V4 = @EMBEDDING_HOST@
    define EMBEDDING_PORT = @EMBEDDING_PORT@

    # GitHub's git-over-SSH endpoints, for `git fetch` as root (install.sh, update.sh).
    # api.github.com/meta "git" (IPv4), 58 entries, re-checked
    # 2026-10-16. A retired address makes `wiki-mirror update` fail with "could not fetch from
    # origin" - the intended tripwire. Root also holds tcp 443 anywhere, so the fallback is
    # `ssh://git@ssh.github.com:443/...` in the checkout's origin URL (docs/OPERATIONS.md).
    set github_git {
        type ipv4_addr
        flags interval
        comment "api.github.com/meta git ranges, 2026-10-16 - root fetches only"
        elements = {
            192.30.252.0/22, 185.199.108.0/22, 140.82.112.0/20, 143.55.64.0/20,
            4.208.26.193, 4.208.26.197, 4.208.26.198,
            4.225.11.194, 4.225.11.199, 4.225.11.200,
            4.228.31.144, 4.228.31.145, 4.228.31.150,
            4.237.22.36, 4.237.22.38, 4.237.22.40,
            4.249.131.163, 4.249.131.164,
            20.26.156.213, 20.26.156.214, 20.26.156.215,
            20.27.177.113, 20.27.177.118, 20.27.177.119,
            20.29.134.19, 20.29.134.22, 20.29.134.23,
            20.87.245.0, 20.87.245.2, 20.87.245.4,
            20.175.192.146, 20.175.192.147,
            20.199.39.227, 20.199.39.232,
            20.200.245.244, 20.200.245.247, 20.200.245.248,
            20.201.28.151, 20.201.28.152,
            20.205.243.160, 20.205.243.161, 20.205.243.166,
            20.207.73.81, 20.207.73.82, 20.207.73.83,
            20.217.135.1, 20.217.135.4, 20.217.135.5,
            20.233.83.145, 20.233.83.148, 20.233.83.149,
            48.202.248.38, 48.202.248.40,
            48.204.201.5, 48.204.201.6,
            172.182.252.130, 172.182.252.133, 172.182.252.135
        }
    }

    # What was refused, readable with `nft list counters` (and by `wiki-mirror status`). Named, so
    # they survive being referenced from several rules and can be reset individually.
    counter in_refused {
        comment "Inbound connections no rule admitted (not SSH or 8300 from the LAN)"
    }
    counter in_invalid {
        comment "Inbound packets conntrack calls invalid"
    }
    counter out_refused {
        comment "Outbound connections no rule admitted - a new egress need or a compromise"
    }
    counter service_refused {
        comment "wikimirror (API) or qdrant tried to open an outbound connection - always an event"
    }

    chain input {
        type filter hook input priority filter; policy drop;

        ct state invalid counter name "in_invalid" drop
        ct state established,related accept
        iifname "lo" accept

        # Echo and unreachables for diagnosis; ICMPv6 is not optional (neighbour discovery,
        # path MTU) and is only ever link-local on this box.
        ip protocol icmp accept
        meta l4proto ipv6-icmp accept

        # SSH from the LAN. The first rule that admits anything new, deliberately above the
        # service rules so a typo below cannot take the operator's way in.
        ip saddr $LAN_V4 tcp dport 22 ct state new accept

        # The API port, from the whole LAN rather than the main client alone: the service also serves
        # an MCP endpoint (/mcp, added after W1) to several agents on the LAN, each with its
        # own key. The firewall is not the access control here - every route requires a
        # bearer key, and the data is public Wikipedia text - so it only keeps the port off
        # anything outside the LAN.
        ip saddr $LAN_V4 tcp dport 8300 ct state new accept

        limit rate 10/minute burst 5 packets log prefix "wiki-mirror-input-drop: " level warn
        counter name "in_refused" drop
    }

    chain output {
        type filter hook output priority filter; policy drop;

        # Replies to the SSH session and to clients' API calls ride on these.
        ct state established,related accept
        oifname "lo" accept

        # The two services that must never dial out. Placed before every generic accept (DNS
        # included) so no later rule can widen what they may do. Counted, then refused with a
        # reset / admin-prohibited so the service sees the failure at once (header). Logged first
        # (rate-limited, like the egress log below): the counter alone said 4 packets after the
        # first build (2026-10-18) and nothing could say where they were going.
        meta skuid { "wikimirror", "qdrant" } limit rate 10/minute burst 5 packets log prefix "wiki-mirror-service-refused: " level warn
        meta skuid { "wikimirror", "qdrant" } meta l4proto tcp counter name "service_refused" reject with tcp reset
        meta skuid { "wikimirror", "qdrant" } counter name "service_refused" reject with icmpx type admin-prohibited

        # Neighbour discovery on the local link (no routed IPv6 is accepted anywhere).
        ip6 daddr { fe80::/10, ff02::/16 } meta l4proto ipv6-icmp accept

        # DNS and NTP to the LAN resolver only. Public resolvers are deliberately not
        # permitted - DNS is a viable exfiltration channel. The resolver must be pinned on the
        # Proxmox side (`pct set <ctid> --nameserver <resolver>`), or nothing resolves.
        ip daddr $LAN_V4 udp dport { 53, 123 } accept
        ip daddr $LAN_V4 tcp dport 53 accept

        # Ping to the LAN (embedding server, NAS, main client) for diagnosis; replies to it are established.
        ip daddr $LAN_V4 icmp type echo-request accept

        # The download/parse/index identity. IPv4 only: Enterprise and S3 are reached over
        # IPv4, and an unrouted ULA must not make the client try IPv6 first and stall.
        meta nfproto ipv4 meta skuid "wikimirror-sync" tcp dport 443 accept
        meta skuid "wikimirror-sync" ip daddr $EMBEDDING_V4 tcp dport $EMBEDDING_PORT accept

        # Root: pip installs, source tarballs, git. HTTP as well as HTTPS for python.org and
        # GitHub's redirects.
        meta nfproto ipv4 meta skuid "root" tcp dport { 80, 443 } accept
        meta skuid "root" ip daddr @github_git tcp dport 22 accept

        # apt, which fetches as its sandbox user `_apt`, not as root (header). HTTP as well as
        # HTTPS because Debian's stock sources are http://deb.debian.org (apt verifies the
        # signatures; the transport is not what is trusted). Same shape as root's rule above.
        meta nfproto ipv4 meta skuid "_apt" tcp dport { 80, 443 } accept

        # DHCP lease renewal (RFC 2131): the client renews at half the lease by a unicast from
        # its port 68 to the server's 67 and, only if that goes unanswered, rebinds by
        # broadcast later. Both leave as root (ifupdown's dhclient), through the normal UDP
        # path this chain filters. Confined to the LAN and the limited broadcast address.
        meta skuid "root" udp sport 68 udp dport 67 ip daddr { $LAN_V4, 255.255.255.255 } accept

        # Everything else is refused, counted and (rate-limited) logged. A refused egress
        # attempt from this host is a security event or a missing rule, never noise. The
        # refusal is a reset or an ICMP admin-prohibited rather than a silent drop, so that a
        # v6 attempt, or any destination nobody listed, fails at once instead of waiting out a
        # 30 s connect timeout.
        limit rate 10/minute burst 5 packets log prefix "wiki-mirror-egress-refused: " level warn
        meta l4proto tcp counter name "out_refused" reject with tcp reset
        counter name "out_refused" reject with icmpx type admin-prohibited
    }

    # This box routes nothing; refusing it explicitly keeps that true if forwarding is ever
    # switched on by accident.
    chain forward {
        type filter hook forward priority filter; policy drop;
    }
}
